Introduction
============

Instructions on how to run NEMO-3D have been given in build/BUILD.
This file briefly describes the capability and basic functionality 
of NEMO-3D.  In brief, NEMO-3D is a tight-binding based electronic
structure solver.  At the moment, only zinc-blende structures have
been implemented, but the code has been written in a sufficiently
general way that it can be readily modified to handle any kind of 
atomic connectivity.  The electronic structure component also
includes postprocessing tools (described in NEMO3D/postprocessing/README)
to compute electron-hole exchange energies and dipole moments.
Electronic structure calculations necessitate computation of atomic
positions.  These are computed via a simple Keating model.  Apart
from its use in the electronic structure calculation, this strain
component of the calculation can be used to compute phonon
dispersions.  This work is still under development, but documentation
for it can be found in NEMO3D/nemo3d/doc/README_phonon.pdf.


Input Parameters
================
The input file is XML-based.  Small dummy examples are provided in 
this directory (nemo3d/examples).  To facillitate input file editing,
a primitive java-based front-end, WIGLAF, has been provided and should
be located in the top-level subdirectory of the root of the NEMO-3D
installation.  Please refer to the WIGLAF/README file for installation
and execution instructions.  [NOTE: There is currently a bug which
prevents proper execution of WIGLAF under bash.  For the moment,
please use (t)csh].  Otherwise, the input file may be edited by hand
(i.e. with vi, emacs, etc.), but this is not recommended.

The meanings of the input file parameters are mostly self-evident.
The values of XML elements tagged by <desc>...</desc> provide a brief
description of the meaning of the input parameter.  The parameters
are organized in a tree-like structure.  The root has three children
corresponding to parameter sets that describe the geometry, allowed
material systems, and execution of the program.  The geometry
parameters describe the physical layout of the system including
material type, boundary conditions, and crystalline structure.
The physical structure is determined by an ordered list of shapes,
consisting of a single material.  The first shape, which must be a box, 
describes the extent of the entire simulation domain.  Only the 
intersections of subsequent shapes with the first shape are retained 
in the calculation.  Also, the material properties of the intersection 
of any two shapes are determined by the shape that is specified latest.
The allowed material systems are represented in the XML input as
an unordered list of materials specified by an atomic basis for the
Bravais lattice and a basis describing the set of orbitals used
(i.e. 20-band, 10-band, etc.).  Finally the execution parameters
dictate the flow of execution of the program including what output
files are to be generated.


Output 
======
Output generated by NEMO-3D consists of files that are typically of
mixed ascii/binary format.  If the input file has the Y.xml, the
generated output is of the form Y.nd_*.  Typical ouputs include
converged eigenvalues, eigenvectors, and atomic positions determined 
from the strain calculation.  

[IMPORTANT NOTE: The determination of eigenvectors is at the moment 
a bit of a black art.  Several numerical solvers are provided, yet 
each has serious shortcomings.  If only eigenvalues are of interest,
the current Lanczos algorithm has proven to be quite accurate, 
provided that an appropriate tolerance is chosen and is probably the 
optimal choice.  However, it does NOT at the moment produce reliable 
eigenvectors.  Eigenvectors determined by ARPACK are reliable, but 
convergence is very slow, does not scale well with problem size, and 
sometimes is not achieved, especially in cases of near-degeneracy
in which the energy spacing between eigenvalues is very small.
Also provided is a Rayleigh-Ritz minimization solver which attempts to
minimize <psi|(H-shift)^2|psi> where shift is chosen to be in the
band gap.  Because the bilinear functional to be minimized is squared,
the condition number is also squared, and so convergence tends to be
rather slow.  Finally a Ritz inverse iteration scheme that uses a
conjugate-gradient based linear solver is also provided.  Convergence
rates tend to be erratic with this solver.]

The capability of transforming the output into human-readable form
is already present within the NEMO-3D executable.  All that needs to
be done is to create a link to the executable whose name is "fmtdat".
That is,
             ln -s <NEMO-3D executable> fmtdat

Then, to convert to/from human-readable format to NEMO-3D format, one
uses fmtdat.  For example to convert to ascii the energy dispersion 
generated by an input file Y.xml, typing

             fmtdat -a2 Y.nd_Ek

would generate a new file Y.nd_Ek_ascii in ascii format.  The switch
"-a2" indicates conversion to ascii from double precision binary.  
Simply invoking fmtdat gives a list of options.


Visualization
=============
Several Matlab functions are provided to enable visualization of the
generated output.  They are located in the viz/matlab_fn subdirectory.
To use them, add this subdirectory to the MATLABPATH environment
variable.  For example in bash:

   export MATLABPATH=${MATLABPATH}:${HOME_NEMO3D}/viz/matlab_fn

To view a list of available functions, type
   
   help matlab_fn

For each function listed, one can view documentation by using the 
same help function.


Examples
========
A small set of examples in nemo3d/example are provided as a template:

Si_bulk.xml:
This example generates the bulk energy dispersion of Si along [100].
This is achieved by imposing periodic boundary conditions on a single 
cubic cell of Si.  Note that since the unit cell that is used is cubic, 
the Brillouin zone of the cube is not the same as that of a primitve
tetrahedral consequently.  Consequently, the generated dispersion
needs to be "stretched out" by a factor of 2.  To view the dispersion,
one needs to run the code as follows

mpirun -np 1 nemo3d.ex Si_bulk.xml   # run code

To view within matlab, one can simply run:

x=readData('Si_bulk.nd_Ek');
plot(x(:,1),x(:,4),'.');

Otherwise, one can run

fmtdat -a2 Si_bulk.nd_Ek

to generate an ascii file Si_bulk.nd_Ek_ascii, which can produces
a human readable ascii file containing the data which may be plotted
with a utility like gnuplot.  After the initial header, the data
are organized into 4 columns consisting of {kx,ky,kz,E(k)}.


InGaAsQD_in_GaAs.xml:
This example find the lowest electron and hole eigenvalues for a very
small dome-shaped InGaAs QD contained within a small buffer of GaAs.
Note that in this very small example, the wave functions are confined not
within the QD, but rather by the buffer.

